1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:04,600
Around 300 BC, Euclid wrote down an algorithm.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,400
Measure the larger by the smaller, then measure again with what is left.

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,800
When nothing remains, the greatest common divisor reveals itself.

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:19,400
Twenty-three centuries later, it still runs at the heart of modern cryptography.

5
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:34,400
Sorting: turning chaos into order.

6
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:39,000
Quicksort: pick a pivot, smaller to the left, larger to the right.

7
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:43,800
The same hundred numbers: bubble sort takes 7,605 steps, quicksort 1,163.

8
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:48,600
Merge sort: with every pass, the sorted runs double in length.

9
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:54,000
Seven passes later, noise has become a spectrum.

10
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:59,400
Ten billion numbers: minutes for quicksort, millennia for bubble sort.

11
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:06,600
Binary search: every step rules out half.

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:11,600
One number in a million, found in twenty steps.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,600
Depth-first: push ahead, and at a dead end, turn back.

14
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,600
Breadth-first: like water, flowing into every branch at once.

15
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,600
The first stream to reach the exit has traced the shortest path.

16
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:36,400
Fourier: any wave can be built from spinning circles.

17
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:40,400
The more circles, the squarer the wave.

18
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:45,400
Three hundred nested circles can write a character.

19
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:50,000
The character is 美: beauty.

20
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:54,400
Sound, images, radio: the world is made of frequencies.

21
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:57,800
The music you are hearing breaks down exactly like this.

22
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:04,400
Each bird follows three rules: separate, align, cohere.

23
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:09,400
No leader, no blueprint, yet a thousand birds move as one.

24
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:13,600
This is emergence: simple rules, complex worlds.

25
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:19,600
In 1952, Turing showed that two reacting, spreading chemicals can grow patterns.

26
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,400
Leopard spots, coral, fingerprints. Perhaps life itself is an algorithm.

27
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:32,400
Recursion: a function that calls itself.

28
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:35,800
Twelve calls deep: four thousand and ninety-six branches.

29
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:40,400
The Mandelbrot set: one line, z becomes z² plus c.

30
00:02:40,800 --> 00:02:45,200
Iterate every point on the plane and ask: does it escape to infinity?

31
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:50,200
The points that never escape form this black island. Its coastline is infinitely complex.

32
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:55,400
We are magnifying it seven billion times.

33
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:59,400
The deeper we go, the more there is.

34
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:03,800
And at the very bottom, it finds itself again.

35
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:10,400
The traveling salesman: visit every city. What is the shortest route?

36
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:15,400
With 1,500 cities, there are more routes than atoms in the universe.

37
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:20,400
Simulated annealing: like cooling metal, bold at first, then slowly settling.

38
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:25,400
In the end, a single line passes through every star.

39
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:31,200
And what it draws is the very Mandelbrot island we just explored.

40
00:03:34,200 --> 00:03:38,600
Gradient descent: follow the steepest slope, step by step, to the valley floor.

41
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,600
Almost every AI today learns this way.

42
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:48,800
Neural networks: the rules are no longer written. They are learned from data.

43
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:53,600
Two intertwined spirals. At first, it can only draw a straight line.

44
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,800
After 150 rounds of training, it learns to bend.

45
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:03,600
Today, algorithms have learned to write, to paint, to code.

46
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:09,600
Even this film, every frame and every note, was computed.

47
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:15,400
Sorting, searching, transforming, emerging, recursing, optimizing, learning:

48
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:20,600
they hide behind every click, every search, every message.

49
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,600
An algorithm is human thought in its most precise form.

50
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,200
Cold and rigorous, and astonishingly beautiful.

51
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:39,000
A finite set of steps,

52
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:44,600
leading to infinite possibility.
